Date: Fri, 07 May 1993 17:47:54 -0400
From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
Subject: The Incredibly High Cost of High Tech 
X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute only with permission

Timothy C. May says:
> If the cyberspace world takes off the way many of us think it will, then
> having $50-100 billion tied up in manufacturing plants seems plausble, if
> staggering. As I've said so many times before, the key is self-financing!!
> 
> The "road to nanotech," to get back to themes of more direct interest to
> this list, must essentially be self-financing, as the chip industry was
> from 1955 onwards. The simple circuits of 1960 funded the R&D and factories
> of 1965 which built the first commercial TTL gates, and those circuits paid
> for the factories of 1970, and so on. (I've made these points before.)
> 
> What's getting the Japanese into trouble now is that they _borrowed_ the
> money to build their DRAM plants and now the loans are due and
> payable...but the profits aren't enough to cover them! (This is a story
> someone needs to write up for a major magazine.)

@Begin(pedagog-mode)

Tim unfortunately is perpetuating a bad meme here -- the "Financing
technique X is the best" meme. In fact, capitalization structure of
large corporate ventures often depends on the prevailing interest
rate, tax laws, prevailing equity market P/E ratios, etc. MCI was
financed with junk bonds by Mike Milken, and was a stunning success.
Other companies have been similarly financed and have been failures.
Many companies have self-financed and been successfull at it. Others
have strangled for lack of capital that selling some debt or equity
would have cured. (Entrepreneurs are often foolish about not wanting
to sell any equity. A key thing to remember is that owning 10% of a
billion dollar company is far better than owning 50% of a 100 million
dollar company. If you can do without the money, as is sometimes the
case, thats fine, but other times you really need the capital...)

Capitalizing a venture is always a per-case sort of thing. It depends
on what you are doing, what sort of assets you are buying (a fab line
is swiftly obsolete -- a power plant lasts for 20 years and is often
best paid for with mortgage bonds), what the prevailing rates are,
etc. Blanket pronouncements are often made by managers who have only
seen one part of a business cycle or one industry -- which is to be
expected, because such managers haven't seen a variety of conditions.
In reality, a variety of capitalization schemes work right depending
on conditions.

@End(pedagog-mode)

Perry

