Subject: The Incredibly High Cost of High Tech

Robert Brooks writes:   (BTW, good to see you here again...it's been a while)

>As a counterpoint, HP has had no such trouble with our similarly complex
>(and higher performance) PA7100 chip.  Our fabs are confident they can
>maintain leading edge capability for the forseeable future at an
>investment level that, while high, is nowhere close to $5G.

I did not mean to imply that $5 billion is what Intel needs to spend to
produce the same number of chips as H-P may be able to build with a much
lower investment! I meant of course that this is the amount of capacity
Intel is building in anticipation of market demands for the Pentium.

The H-P PA chip is a fine chip, with excellent performance. Ditto for the
MIPS R4000 (now owned by Silicon Graphics). The SPARC is seriously lagging,
and the DEC Alpha may be too expensive to ever be a serious competitor to
the other high-volume chips. About the Motorola-Apple-IBM PowerPC I am also
skeptical, as Moto has really fallen behind in fab technology and capacity.

Will the Intel chips dominate? I don't know. Polls indicate most users will
upgrade to a faster 486 or a Pentium system before they switch to another
processor. Generally, a 2x speed improvement is not worth a
platform/architecture change (which is one reason Sun is still in business,
despite lagging in the CPU area).

Windows NT, Solaris 2.0, NeXTStep, and even "EBM" ("Everybody but
Microsoft," the Apple, Novell, IBM, SCO, etc. consortium) may indeed make
the choice of a CPU less critical.

I won't sell all my Intel stock yet, though, as the production capacity now
being built (and paid for out of current earnings, importantly) can of
course be used for whatever architecture is selling (modulo licensing...).
Intel also builds the most successful RISC chip, the 960, and a
less-successful scientific processing chip, the 860. Various other chips to
be built on the 0.6 micron process are of course already being sold or are
being designed.

