 rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu) writes:
>  Is there a similar fate in store for human brains? It is generally
>acknowledged that the young learn faster than the old. Will living
>200+ years cause your brain to be so bogged down and clouded
>that you will be unable to keep up with the world? Is insanity
>an eventual result of long life?  For those in neural net
>research, isn't there a problem with neural nets screwing up if
>you try to teach them too much?

 There are very definite limits on how much information an
individual neuron can store. How close a typical human mind comes
to this limit is probably impossible to guess with our current
understanding of the brain. I suspect most difficulties with
learning new concepts result from a strategy of learning new
information in proportion to its "surprise value", rather than
hard limits on storage capacities.

 Since it took a lot of evolutionary selection to kludge up a
brain that works for 100 years and there has been no evolutionary
pressure directly influencing the functioning of a 200 year old
brain, it would not be the least bit surprising if some accidental
byproducts of our evolutionary history caused some problems with 200
year old brains.

>  AI and information technology are an integral part of 
>Extropian interests. Immortality may require more than just
>a materialistic-cellular solution, it may require uploading.

 Since I assume that any human brain that lives for 200 years will be
hopelessly obsolete compared to an uploaded mind, I don't think that
any obstacles to immortality caused by our current biology are likely
to matter.

