From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: ContrAnton

Anton Sherwood chided me for by brash attack on Kant.  I assure you all, I
wasn't making up a straw man.  Anyone that can prove me wrong based on his
Critique of Pure Reason will have my thanks.

What I was really responding to were the equally brash claims that so many
parts of Objectivism are so easily discredited.  The sort of thing I have
in mind is the claim that the notion that humans are born tabula rasa is
passe.  Since when have any babies been born conversant with how to tell
edible mushrooms from poisonous ones, or Einstein's E=MC2, or the sort of
evolutionary theory that I presume this claim is based upon?  Why haven't
I heard of the studies in which these results have been published?

Since I do subscribe to Jimbo Wales' list for the discussion of Objectivism,
and have experience with that more elevated criticism and affirmation of
diverse parts thereof, it's hard for such extreme claims to get anything but
contempt out of me.  There may indeed be cases to be made for them; I haven't
seen it even attempted on this list.

As for straw men, I could as easily make the same accusation myself, but what
seems to be the case is merely honest error.  The mind-body dichotomy is one
of the great mistakes of Western philosophy, and merits much criticism.  I
just don't think Aristotle deserves most of the blame for it.  What F.A.
Hayek called "constructivist rationalism" falls into the same category.  What
I took issue with about Ray Cromwell's post wasn't so much the ideas he
took as his foils as who he attributed them to.

While I'm being miscellaneous, I've thought more about Stanton McAndlish's
posts about ethics and morals.

One reason why those who speak of ethics rather than morals may advocate more
universal things is that they've had to study an historically longer school
of thought: all the ethicists since the Ancients.

Those who speak of morals, rather than morals, seem on the basis of anecdotal
evidence to study little more than the Old Testament of the Bible, at least
in my culture (and that oughta say: "rather than ethics" above).  Seeking truth
and justice in more than one source would yield this effect.

That's all for now.

