have to be taught to suckle (after having the necessity of milk
consumption explained to them), unlike on Earth where, funnily enough,
no such coaching is necessary.

Such arguments are not necessary because I make the simple observation
that humans are animals.  Most animals are born in a highly hard-wired
state, able to run with the herd or scratch for grubs or suckle within
minutes of birth.  Humans are animals, ergo....  The proposition is
really on the tabula rasa adherents to demonstrate their case, rather
than the reverse.  Until I see convincing evidence that humans are not
animals I shall make the modest minimalist assumption that we are.

On the conceptual level note that humans have an innate concept of other
people and minds.  Children in which this innate concept is not hard-
wired develop as autistic cripples.  They have to *learn* that other
people have internal mental states thence develop communication skills
etc.  Autistic children have such problems with language because they
don't realise they other people are seeing the world from a different
perspective.  This additional hard-wiring is what distinguishes the apes
from the monkeys.

On the neurophysiological level note that even as an embryo the brain
is active.  The culling of superfluous neurons and pathways and the
developing of new routes starts in the womb.  Witness the firing of
retinal cells in embryos and the associated development of structures
in the visual cortex.  So reminiscent of neural networks, I might add.

