Date: Sat, 8 May 93 13:39:59 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: LIFE-X: the 120 yr limit, cryogenics, blah

> >Peter McCluskey writes:

> >Is that right?  Since these limits are "very definite", would
> >you mind saying what they are?

>  For an artificial neural network, there is a limit of 2 times the number
> of inputs on the number of vectors that a network can distinguish. See:
> John Hertz, Anders Krogh, and Richard G. Palmer, Introduction to
> the Theory of Neural Computation, Addison-Wesley 1991, section 5.7
> for more details.

We're talking neurons in our brains.  That result you quote is
for *perceptrons* (linear threshold units), the simplest possible
[inadequate, clearly] neurally-inspired devices.  Claiming that
theoretical results for perceptrons apply to biological
neurons is simply wrong.  Just consider the following:  the
reason that "the number of vectors that a network [sic, actually
only 2-layer networks with small numbers of output units] can
distinguish" is even expressible is that perceptrons have a finite
(small) number of output states.  Continuous-valued outputs (such
as the sigmoid units most commonly trained with backpropagation) can
have arbitrarly many different ranges we could call "distinguishing".
Biological neurons have an unknown capacity for different output
values -- but it's probably VERY large.  You'd better choose a different
way to measure memory than that!

Besides not being threshold units, real neurons do not operate
on linear sums of their inputs, which adds yet another level
of complexity to the function that a biological neuron can compute.

>  I claim that artificial neural networks have been sufficiently successful
> at explaining the behavior of single neurons that we can assume with a
> fair degree of confidence that real neurons have similar storage capacities.

This claim is simply unjustifiable.  ANNs admittedly behave in
ways that are crudely analogous to biological neurons, but that's
about all.  Look at the series of NIPS (Neural Information Processing
Systems) proceedings, not to mention the harder to follow actual
research in neurophysiology, and you can't help but see the layers
of complexity continually being uncovered in neural behavior.

I'm not claiming that neurons have huge storage capacities; it
seems unlikely to me.  But don't try to apply perceptron or
simple sigmoid unit theoretical results to biological molecular
machinery.

A good start would be to model the nervous systems of very simple
organisms (there's a project here at UW to do that for a worm
with about 200 neurons).  The neural models they use get more and
more complex and still last I heard there were anomolies in the
worm's behavior that they simply couldn't explain with their
model.

I hope that there does indeed turn out to be a rather simple,
succinct model of the important parts of neural function.  But
as yet we don't know what's an "important part".

derek

