Subject: LIFE-X: cryonics, limits, etc.

Ray Cromwell writes:

>It is generally
>acknowledged that the young learn faster than the old. Will living
>200+ years cause your brain to be so bogged down and clouded
>that you will be unable to keep up with the world? Is insanity
>an eventual result of long life?  For those in neural net
>research, isn't there a problem with neural nets screwing up if
>you try to teach them too much?

I remember discussing this some time back on a thread called "Creatures of
infinite habit".  The problem as I see it is that biological brains proceed
through a cycle of plasticity and stability - and that this seems to be tied
into the very nature of their process of cognition (a speculation).  Thus if
one wanted to live a very long time, he/she would have to rejuvenate their
brain every 100 years or so to keep it from stabilizing excessively.  This
would in the extreme be an erasing of memories (identity?).  However, perhaps
it will be possible to introduce a new complete cognitive system into a mature
brain and to have the two grow into each other through a competition for
behavioral 'territory'.  (I don't know about the idea, but it brings up an
interesting image of a "non-discrete" identity, perhaps complete even with an
extra peripheral nervous system, perhaps even extra limbs).

>  The hard part is wiring the brain in the new body. The neurosystem 
>of the clone may not exactly match the brain's wiring even if the
>DNA is the same. I think nerve growth largely a fractal process
>and not completely encoded in the DNA. The clone may have more, or less,
>nerve pathways than the original body. The brain and body
>may have to "relearn" each other. If the "weights" of the neural net in the
>body's nervous sysrem start off initialized, then the brain may not
>be able to communicate with the nervous system -- in a sense, the new
>nervous system speaks a different language.

This is somewhat related.  The difficulty in placing an old brain in a new body
is that the old brain has already passed through its stage of sensory and motor
development and may not accept a new set of limbs to control.  Thus, it may be
necessary to make the transition 'less drastic' in some way, perhaps by an
incorporation with a young identity, as above.  Then again, something akin to
the therapy given to stroke patients may be sufficient...

>  And what about the nervous system's information? How much of our
>body's reflexes and coordination are stored outside of the brain.
>If the body is similar to the subsumption architecture that cyberneticists
>use, then a lot of the information that we learn, like walking,
>balancing, running, grasping, etc is actually stored in the nervous
>system in the limbs themselves.

There is a lot of evidence suggesting that this is true.  For example, when a
cat that has had its spinal chord seperated from its brain is suspended over a
moving treadmill, its feet won't just drag over the surface but exhibit a very
stereotypical cat gait.  Furthermore, when an obstacle hits the top of one of
its paws, the cat will actively step over it.  Many things like posture may
result from local reactions to mechanical forces guided by proprioceptive
feedback (like the stretch reflex which is purely spinal), and only later be
replaced by a feedforward control, perhaps through the cerebellum.

>  I hope that these problems are easy, but as you can see, nanotech
>may be the easiest part of the problem.  AI and neuropsychology may
>be needed to understand more about how the nervous system and the brain
>work.

These problems (including the vision issue you mention in your post) are
excruciatingly difficult.  Nanotech will be the easy part!  ;-)

-Paul


